## 11. THINGS THAT WENT WRONG — DO NOT REPEAT
===============================================================================

- THREADING THE ENCODER MADE IT 1.5-5x SLOWER. Tried splitting the time axis
  across 4 threads. The Nord is big.LITTLE (2x A76 + 6x A55); chunks land on
  efficiency cores and the join waits for the slowest. Reverted; a comment in
  SwipeNet.kt records this so nobody retries it.
- 8-WAY vs 4-WAY REGISTER BLOCKING DISAGREES BETWEEN DESKTOP AND PHONE.
  8-way is SLOWER on x86 (13.2 vs 11.2 ms, only 16 FP registers) but FASTER on
  ARM (73 vs 80 ms, 32 FP registers). Benchmark on the phone, not the desktop.
- `adb shell ime set` FAILS on this OnePlus: SecurityException, uid 2000 lacks
  WRITE_SECURE_SETTINGS (OplusInputMethodManagerService). Cannot switch IME
  programmatically. WORKAROUND THAT WORKS: launch MainActivity, tap "Choose
  Keyboard" at (394, 1113), then tap "Compose Keyboard" at (480, 1884).
  REINSTALLING THE APK RESETS THE DEFAULT IME BACK TO GBOARD — redo this after
  every install or the keyboard under test is not yours.
- Multi-point gestures: `adb shell input swipe` only does straight lines. Use
  `adb shell "input motionevent DOWN x y; input motionevent MOVE ...; UP x y"`.
  Working 't->h->e' gesture:
    DOWN 485 1793; MOVE 520 1830; MOVE 570 1880; MOVE 650 1928;
    MOVE 560 1880; MOVE 420 1830; MOVE 271 1793; UP 271 1793
- My synthesizer's noise was calibrated circularly at first — 35% of gestures
  missed a letter by more than the decoder's corridor. Fixed by making
  sloppiness a single dial and reporting a CURVE, not a point. Do not tune noise
  until a decoder looks good.
- I misread the app layout twice: row 2 DOES have 8dp Spacers either side
  (alpha mode only), and rows have three different key widths (row1 106.88px,
  row2 113.75px, row3 110.18px at 1080px width). Verified against
  KeyboardScreen.kt lines ~376-393. `ml/swipe/keymap.py` now matches.
- Kotlin `Array.copyOf(n)` leaves NULLS in the new region; `requireNoNulls()`
  then throws. Bit me in SwipeBeam.build's grow().
- A bad test read `d.words[0]` next to `lab[0]` where `lab` came from a RANDOM
  index batch — looked like a data corruption bug, was not. Offsets were fine.

